{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403821 1072 #uuid "884fc6a3-6df8-5fa0-a711-f28bd28480fb" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403821 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403822 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403823 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [