{aligned}]" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403827 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403827 1084 #uuid "3d0496f0-a286-58c3-82dc-168d307fbe1a" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403829 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403828 1069 "q-75288" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403828 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403828 1072 #uuid "f065fb78-5c08-5a67-9bfa-56a34450ed2a" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403828 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403830 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403831 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403832 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403833 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403834 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1077 "selection" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1079 #edb/ref 17592186403832 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403829 1080 #uuid "bbc1b61e-03fa-5261-ae12-a9f784057a88" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403830 1081 "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)-1" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403830 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403830 1084 #uuid "d5eb255d-3cfa-5ae7-b5a0-ab9f9f6b8e45" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403831 1081 "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)+1" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403831 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403831 1084 #uuid "05bb81c4-43fa-5409-88e4-4f5ef499dd13" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403832 1081 "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403832 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403832 1084 #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403833 1081 "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x-2)+1" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403833 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403833 1084 #uuid "9555e468-7e78-5b3e-afb9-8e76c044cd31" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403834 1081 "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)-1" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403834 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403834 1084 #uuid "13841314-a76c-5620-906a-428ce704c6f4" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403836 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403835 1069 "q-75583" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403835 1071 "Let $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}.$ Find ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ given the function $g$ is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403835 1072 #uuid "8b31d2f7-a6e0-5ec4-9743-3d458a957ec1" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403835 1074 "The function $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}$ is continuous on $[0,x],$ where $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =x\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403836 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403837 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403836 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403838 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403836 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403839 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403836 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403840 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403836 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403841 13194139533493 17592186045585 true] [17592186403836 1076 #edb/ref 175921